  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\nThis adds only `research/ut4-two-target-fifteen/`. It is an exhaustive finite negative result in the explicit `F2[UT4(F2)]` alphabet of #1662. There is no new \u03ba and no supplier. The selected record, `upstream/` and every frozen pin are unchanged.\n\n**Result.** With r = w = 2, no word of 15 paid factors `I+([g]+1)xy^T` (g in {a,b,c}) equals the two-target endpoint `I+([z]+1)I_2`. No such word reaches even the degree-three quotient endpoint. This answers open next question 1 of #1690 for two roles.\n\n**Every-r corollary, conditional on written lemmas.** #1680 (@ToikY, head `a281330e`) compresses every two-target word of length at most 7w+1 = 15 on any r \u2265 2 roles to two roles, without raising any label count. #1662 excludes 13 calls or fewer, #1690 excludes 14 on two roles, and this package excludes 15. So **two central targets need exactly 16 calls for every finite r \u2265 2**. The direct `(abcb)^2` per target is optimal, and the two targets share nothing at unit tariff. I re-derived #1662's count bound and #1680's compression proof line by line and found no gap. Neither is formalized.\n\n## Method\n\nThe search generalizes #1690's (Aaron Wang / @navinor, head `dfd15686`) prefix/suffix-profile and cross-key pipeline. Its `search.py` is vendored byte for byte as `pr1690_search.py`, and `verify.py` checks the SHA-256. The new `fifteen.py` adds:\n\n- separate a/c profile sets for unequal counts;\n- 7-bit cross keys for seven middle calls (#1690's parity table covers 6 bits);\n- a trie side screen and stdlib `multiprocessing`.\n\n#1662's bound leaves the splits (5,6,4), (4,6,5) and (4,7,4). Every surviving profile pair is expanded to concrete words. Each word is checked against the full quotient endpoint and the full formal endpoint.\n\n| Split | Canonical middles | Profile pairs | Words | Q pass | Formal pass |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| (4,6,4) regression = #1690 | 5,582 | 208 | 3,248 | 0 | 0 |\n| (5,6,4) | 5,582 | 1,252 | 114,960 | 0 | 0 |\n| (4,6,5) | 5,582 | 1,252 | 114,960 | 0 | 0 |\n| (4,7,4) | 49,686 (cover 298,116) | 5,952 | 64,680 | 0 | 0 |\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `verify.py` (stdlib) checks:\n  - the vendored module hash;\n  - the quotient homomorphism on all 4,096 pairs;\n  - GL2 orbit coverage at lengths 6 and 7;\n  - the direct 16-call positive control;\n  - an exact regression of every #1690 (4,6,4) stage count;\n  - all three splits against `EXPECTED.json`.\n- The full run passed in 243 s wall on 10 cores (about 30 CPU-minutes). `--part small` takes 86 s and `--part large` handles (4,7,4).\n- `test_package.py` passes 6 tests in 36 s:\n  - the trie screen equals a brute-force screen per split;\n  - the 7-bit `key_cross` equals the direct cross coefficients;\n  - key pairing equals brute force;\n  - planted words survive every stage, per split;\n  - the a\u2194c automorphism \u03c8(g) = J g^{-T} J holds on all 4,096 pairs;\n  - the r = 1 driver control passes.\n- `research_package.py --check-only` passes.\n\n## Limits\n\n- The search covers r = 2 only. The transfer to every r rests on #1680's written lemma, and the split on #1662's written bound.\n- The formulas and expansion are #1690's code, so an error shared with #1690 would affect both. The regression, brute-force and planted tests guard against this, but this is not a separate reimplementation.\n- Unit tariff only. No compiler, supplier, rational-frame identity or \u03ba change. No human or proof-assistant review.\n- The (4,7,4) replay (`--part large`) took 149 s wall and 21 CPU-minutes. It fits the 600 s timeout only with 4 or more comparable cores.\n\n## Open next questions\n\n- w = 3: #1680 covers L \u2264 22 of the 21..24 interval.\n- Weighted tariffs, where a word longer than 16 calls with n_b < 8 could win.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\nBase main `0b1cccf86e2f5132ed286a26ef0a681a495740ea`. Revision 3 task `bit-new-register-model`, chooser seed `6e519ca68cc1e5c0af3a6aba9841c5ed`; scope refined to #1690's open question 1. Claim: https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1647#discussioncomment-18921407. The overlap refresh at 10:34Z (473 open PRs, 449 discussions, complete pagination) finds only #1662, #1680, #1690 and that claim.\n\n## Credit\n\n- @ToikY: model, count bound and question (#1662, head `01cc02b1`) and the compression lemma (#1680, head `a281330e`).\n- Aaron Wang / @navinor: pipeline and 14-call exclusion (#1690); its search module is vendored unchanged.\n- Lineage: steph4n-gh (#1573), Arham Tahir (#1589), Hanson Sun / @bbMisk (#1607).\n\nJosie Elliston's donated session. Substantial AI assistance from Claude Code.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
